## Managed by Ansible (roles/insider_threat_host_audit). Do not hand-edit -
## changes will be overwritten on the next playbook run.
##
## auditd runs on the Docker HOST, not inside dmz-ubuntu - the kernel's
## audit netlink control plane requires the caller to be in the init PID
## namespace, which no container satisfies (confirmed by direct test: a
## privileged container gets "Operation not permitted" on every netlink
## control call even with CAP_AUDIT_CONTROL/CAP_AUDIT_WRITE present).
##
## Scoped to the three insider-threat test account UIDs only
## ({{ insider_threat_test_uid_min }}-{{ insider_threat_test_uid_max }}).
## Deliberately UID-only, no -w <path> / -F exe=<path>: auditctl resolves
## those against the HOST's filesystem at rule-load time, but the commands
## we care about only exist inside dmz-ubuntu's own mount namespace (a
## different inode even for an identically-named host path, if one
## happened to exist). The executable path IS still usable downstream,
## because the kernel's d_path() records audit.exe/audit.cwd relative to
## the *executing task's own* mount namespace - confirmed live, so Wazuh's
## rules can safely match audit.exe even though this rule itself cannot.
##
## One shared key for all matched execve - command-level classification
## (whoami vs nmap vs ssh vs ...) happens in the Wazuh rule layer via a
## positive match on audit.exe/audit.command, not here, since per-command
## auditd rules would all share the identical UID-only match condition
## anyway (nothing here can distinguish by command).
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -F auid>={{ insider_threat_test_uid_min }} -F auid<={{ insider_threat_test_uid_max }} -k insider_threat_exec
